<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Photisarath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1501">1501</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Photisarath I, Phothisarath</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Photisarath was king of Lan Xang from 1520 to 1547, a devout Buddhist who in 1527 ordered the suppression of spirit cults in favour of Buddhist practice. He married a princess of Lan Na, which allowed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> to claim the Lan Na throne, and he died in an accident while capturing an elephant.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/photisarath.jpg" alt="Photisarath" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kittipong khunnen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phra_That_Phanom_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Photisarath was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visoun">Visoun</a> of Lan Xang, who reigned from 1500 to 1520; his mother's name is not known. He is considered the most devout of the Lao kings. In 1533 he married Queen Yot Kam Tip, also called Nang Nhot-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a>, a daughter of Mueangketklao, king of Lan Na at Chiang Mai, and he took several other consorts, among them a princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. His sons included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a>, Phra Lancharng, Phra Vorawong and Vorawongse I, who reigned in Lan Xang from 1575 to 1579.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His 1527 decree called the worship of spirits groundless superstition and ordered their shrines destroyed and their altars thrown into the river, and he built temples on the sites of spirit shrines. He worked to undermine Brahmanic practices as well as animism. He lived much of the time not in the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, but in Vientiane, farther south, which had better communications with the main states of the region.</p><p>His reign drew Lan Xang into the wars of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, he sent campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a> in 1535 and to Vieng Prangarm in 1539. In 1540 King Chairachathirat of Ayutthaya invaded with a large army, took Muang Khouk and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, but was routed at the Battle of Sala Kham, his army leaving enormous casualties behind. When Mueangketklao died in 1546 without a male heir, Photisarath's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> was placed on the throne of Chiang Mai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Photisarath's death, his sons divided the kingdom. Phra Vorawong seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and the lands north of Chiengkarn, and Phra Lancharng took those to the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> returned from Chiang Mai, expelled Phra Vorawong and joined the two kingdoms under his rule. Phra Lancharng was defeated and taken prisoner, then pardoned and made governor of Saenmuong. The union was soon caught up in the Burmese–Siamese wars that devastated much of the region over the next half-century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Photisarath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Monasteries were centres of education and literacy in the Lao script, preserving religious texts, chronicles and literature on palm leaves, and King <b>Photisarath</b> ordered the suppression of spirit cults in favour of Buddhist practice in 1527.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. Died in Vientiane, in what is now <a href="/countries/laos">Laos</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1530" aria-label="Luang Prabang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7306 1427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Photisarath"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7756 1697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7756 1697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.89,102.14&amp;year=1530">What was here in 1530? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
